#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a03ad88-dc53-582c-b2ed-1aeb0ee2d0f5" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Binomial Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4879d9-6205-5c3e-94e2-345d32f9bb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263124} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb4e03d-474f-5887-8f27-c908a061ad15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263291} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba2368b-f5a3-5378-8bc6-6b7ddf0dd2f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{243}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263652} {:answer/id #uuid "66ad7f63-b3d8-5c17-bd92-a48eb9d8c999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263888} {:answer/id #uuid "d85db2a7-c7b1-56c0-8c12-5c87f1f638ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb4e03d-474f-5887-8f27-c908a061ad15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263291} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3af6c4a-5163-5b84-8a2a-a422d73546a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68512d61-c123-5b4f-bd6e-2abe384cfbfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-129971" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ compute $P(X<3).$" :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {0,1,2}) \\\\  & =f(0)+f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}+4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1882e22f-edeb-58a1-a73e-a5b295cb9ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1882e22f-edeb-58a1-a73e-a5b295cb9ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293030} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee4bd34-7a14-524f-aa57-4ed60dbfe284" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "974e2ad3-54a9-51be-bbda-c3b480d74b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-279981" :question/problem "If $X∼B(6,0.9),$ then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(X<5)≈${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X<5)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,3,4.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X\\ge 5)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=5,6,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X\\ge 5),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.9)^{5}(0.1)+(0.9)^{6} \\\\  & ≈0.886\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =1-P(X\\ge 5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.886 \\\\  & ≈0.114\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
